// 20 stories from the Sunset Dreams Lounge.
window.SUNSET_STORIES = [
 {
  "title": "The Back Booth",
  "body": "Renata came in the first time because the hospital cafeteria closed at midnight and the Sunset Dreams Lounge did not. She ordered tea. The bartender laughed, kindly, and made her one anyway in a rocks glass with a lemon wheel, and that was how Vincent noticed her.\n\nHe did not come over that night. He came over the fourth night, after she had stopped bringing a book. He set down a plate of olives she had not ordered and said, \"Night shift,\" not a question. She asked how he knew. He touched his own wrist where her watch was, the cheap rubber kind nurses wear because they get washed so often. \"My mother,\" he said. \"Thirty years at Mercy.\"\n\nShe did not check. Why would she check.\n\nThe back booth was the only one with a curtain. Regulars talked about it the way people talk about a table at a famous restaurant, half joking, half not. On the second Thursday in November Vincent held the curtain open for her and said she looked like someone who needed to sit somewhere nobody could ask her for anything. Inside it was warm and smelled of cedar. There was a lamp with a fringed shade. He did not sit down with her. That was the clever part, she would think much later. He let her have it alone.\n\nAfter that the booth was hers on Thursdays. The bartender, Ines, would catch her eye when she came in and tilt her head toward the curtain, and Renata felt a little foolish and a little chosen, which is a combination that is hard to give up.\n\nHe started joining her in December. Ten minutes, then twenty. He asked about patients and remembered their names. He remembered Mr. Abernathy, who wanted his dog brought in, and asked a week later if the dog had come. No one at home asked her about Mr. Abernathy. Her husband had stopped asking about the hospital around the time she stopped asking about his job, an even trade.\n\n\"You give everything away,\" Vincent said one night. \"Who gives anything to you?\"\n\nShe said nobody, as a joke. He did not laugh. He just nodded, slowly, like a doctor writing something down.\n\nBy January she was telling him things she had not told her husband. That she was thinking of leaving hospice. That she sometimes sat in the parking garage for forty minutes before going inside her own house. He listened with his whole face. Once, when she cried, he did not hand her a napkin. He waited until she reached for one herself, and then said, \"There she is.\"\n\nIn February she came in on a Tuesday, because things at home were bad, and the curtain on the back booth was closed. She could hear a woman's voice behind it, soft, laughing at something. Ines would not look at her.\n\nVincent came out ten minutes later and found her at the bar. He put his hand on her shoulder, warm, and said, quietly enough that only she could hear, \"Thursdays, sweetheart. You know that.\"\n\nShe did know that. She found she was grateful he had reminded her."
 },
 {
  "title": "On the House",
  "body": "You made partner on a Wednesday and nobody had planned anything, which was fine, which you told yourself was fine, so you walked eleven blocks in the rain to the only neon you could see. The Sunset Dreams Lounge. The sign buzzed like it was clearing its throat.\n\nYou ordered the most expensive scotch on the shelf because you could. The man at the end of the bar, silver at the temples, sleeves rolled, told the bartender to put it on the house. \"Congratulations,\" he said. You had not told anyone. \"You've got the look,\" he said. \"First time you've won something you can't explain to your parents.\"\n\nThat was Ray. He owned the place. He said it like an apology.\n\nYou came back Friday. The scotch was on the house again. Ray had saved you the stool by the window, a little card on it that said RESERVED in gold script. He asked about the firm, the cases, the senior partner who called you kiddo. He laughed in the right places. He said, \"They're scared of you. That's what kiddo means.\"\n\nYou had never thought of it that way. You thought of it that way for weeks.\n\nIt was always on the house. You tried to pay and he put his hand flat over your card on the bar and slid it back to you, and the gesture had a weight to it, a pleasant weight, like a coat someone sets on your shoulders. Your friends from law school came once. Ray was cool to them. Not rude. Just cool. Afterward he said, \"They talk about you like you're still twenty-four,\" and you realized they did.\n\nThey did not come again. You did not invite them.\n\nIn March you brought a client. Ray comped the whole table and sent over oysters, and the client said you must be someone here, and you felt it in your chest. Afterward, at the bar, Ray slid a small leather folder toward you. Inside was a handwritten ledger. Every drink since November. Every oyster. The total was underlined twice.\n\n\"Don't look like that,\" he said, smiling. \"I'm not asking for it. I just keep things. I keep everybody's things.\" He closed the folder and tucked it into his jacket. \"It's good to know where we stand with each other.\"\n\nYou said you would pay it. He looked hurt. He said that would insult him. He said you were the only person in this room he trusted, and that he wanted to ask you something, just a question about a lease, nothing, something a lawyer could look at over a drink.\n\nYou looked at it over a drink. Then over several. The questions got larger. You began billing your hours at the firm a little thinner to make time.\n\nIn June the senior partner called you kiddo and you did not feel anything at all. You were thinking about Ray, whether he had saved the stool, whether he was annoyed with you for coming in late on Tuesday.\n\nHe was. He did not say so. He just let you pay, for the first time, and watched you sign."
 },
 {
  "title": "Residency",
  "body": "My agent said two weeks at the Sunset Dreams Lounge was beneath me, and she was right, and I took it anyway because the rent on my apartment does not care what is beneath me.\n\nDorian met me at the stage door with a space heater. That was the first thing. A little ceramic heater, plugged in already, aimed at the stool where I'd sit between sets. \"Singers get cold,\" he said. \"Nobody thinks about it.\" In nineteen years of clubs nobody had.\n\nThe room was small and amber and smelled like orange peel. The piano was tuned. The light he gave me was perfect, a single warm spot that made my skin look like I'd slept. I sang better than I had in a year. He stood at the back, by the coat check, every set, and never once looked at his phone.\n\nAfter the first week he asked if I'd stay a third. Then a fourth. He said the room had changed since I came. He said, \"They don't come for the drinks anymore. They come for you.\" I knew better than to believe that and I believed it.\n\nThere were things. Small things. My keyboard player, Hollis, wanted a Saturday off for his daughter's recital, and Dorian said of course, and then on Saturday the sound was off and the light was wrong, too blue, and three tables walked out, and Dorian found me afterward and said, gently, \"It's not the same without the band you trust, is it.\" I let Hollis go the next week. Dorian found me a new player. A kid who did exactly what he was told.\n\nHe started coming to my dressing room before shows. He'd sit in the doorway, one foot in the hall, always one foot in the hall. He'd tell me which song to open with. He was always right. He'd tell me which dress. He was usually right. He told me my agent didn't understand my voice, that she was selling me like a product, and I fired her in January and let him handle the bookings, which mostly meant there weren't any outside the lounge.\n\nI don't remember deciding to stop going home between sets. My apartment was forty minutes away and there was a cot in the storeroom and he'd put a quilt on it, a nice one, hand stitched. \"My grandmother's,\" he said.\n\nMy contract was up in March. I asked him about renewal and he looked at me with real sorrow and said the owners were cutting costs. I said, you're the owner. He said, \"Not of everything.\"\n\nI sang that night like I was begging. The spotlight felt hot. He stood by the coat check and did not look at his phone, and at the end of the last set he gave a single slow clap, alone, until the room joined in.\n\nHe renewed me the next morning, at half the rate, and I thanked him. I actually thanked him. I hugged him in the doorway and he patted my back twice, the way you pat a dog that has finally learned to sit."
 },
 {
  "title": "What I Saw from the Well",
  "body": "From behind the bar at the Sunset Dreams Lounge you can see every booth reflected in the mirror, if you know how to tilt your head. I have worked the well for six years. I know how to tilt my head.\n\nThe woman with the laptop started coming in September. Thirty, maybe, a doctoral student named Meredith, writing about medieval Portuguese trade routes, which she explained to me once, generously, while I cut limes. She took the corner booth by the outlet and drank seltzer with bitters and tipped like someone who had once worked a bar.\n\nLyle noticed her in the third week. I saw him notice. He has a way of going still.\n\nHe did not approach her. He told me to start bringing her the seltzer before she asked. He told me to turn the music down a notch when she was typing. When the outlet in her booth stopped working, it was because he had me flip the breaker, and when she asked about it, he came over himself with an extension cord and a little apology and stayed four minutes. I timed it. He always stays four minutes the first time.\n\nThen he started reading. I would find books on the end of the bar, history books, Portuguese ones. He left them where she would see. He asked her one night, as if he'd just thought of it, whether she knew anything about the spice trade.\n\nI watched her face open like a window.\n\nBy November they were talking an hour at a time. He is a good listener; that is not the lie. He let her explain things to him he already knew. He found her an archive, a private collector, letters. He knew a man. He always knows a man. The man would only meet at the lounge, after close.\n\nI was not on those nights. Lyle changed the schedule.\n\nIn December her advisor emailed her something unkind, and she cried in the booth, and Lyle sat across from her and said the advisor was threatened, said the department did not deserve her, said maybe she didn't need them. I saw her nod. I saw her close the laptop, and she did not open it again in the lounge after that. She came to talk. Only to talk.\n\nIn February she asked me, while I was cutting limes, whether I thought Lyle was a good man. She asked it lightly. She had stopped tipping like someone who'd worked a bar. She had stopped paying at all.\n\nI tilted my head toward the mirror and saw him at the end of the bar, watching us. Not angry. Just still.\n\nI said yes. I said he was very generous.\n\nShe smiled like I'd given her something. Then she went back to her booth, and Lyle came down the bar and put a twenty in my tip jar, folded once, the way he always does when I've done well."
 },
 {
  "title": "We, the Regulars",
  "body": "We noticed Albert before Marcus did, or we like to think so. He came in the winter after his wife died, a tall man in a camel coat who sat close to the piano and didn't drink what he ordered. We knew the type. Some of us had been the type.\n\nHe came for the piano. Sundays, the house player did standards, and Albert would mouth the words to \"Skylark\" with his eyes closed. We left him alone. That is the courtesy of the Sunset Dreams Lounge. Nobody asks.\n\nMarcus asked.\n\nWe watched him do it, one Sunday in February, carrying a drink Albert hadn't ordered and setting it down with two fingers like a chess move. He said Ruth used to request that song. We looked at one another. None of us had told him about Ruth. Albert had never said her name aloud in that room.\n\nLater we figured out it was the obituary. Marcus reads them. He keeps a stack behind the register, clipped, and some of us knew that and some of us didn't, and all of us would have said it was sweet, before.\n\nAlbert began coming Wednesdays too. Then Fridays. Marcus moved the piano a foot closer to his table and told the house player to learn the rest of Ruth's songs, which Marcus somehow knew, all of them. Albert cried during \"Moonglow\" and Marcus sat with him, and we turned our faces politely toward the bar.\n\nAlbert's son came in once, in April. A heavy, worried man. He asked Albert to come to dinner. Marcus brought the son a drink on the house and talked to him about grief, about how everyone grieves differently, about how some fathers need a place that isn't home. The son left alone. Albert watched him go and said nothing, and Marcus put a hand on his shoulder and said, \"He means well.\"\n\nWe all heard the shape of that sentence. We didn't say so.\n\nIn June Albert gave Marcus the piano. Ruth's piano, a Steinway from their living room, delivered on a Tuesday by three men in gray uniforms. Marcus said he couldn't possibly accept it, and accepted it, and had a little brass plate made: IN MEMORY OF RUTH. The house player plays it now. It sounds better than the old one.\n\nAlbert stopped coming in September. We assumed he had died, or moved in with his son. Marcus said he'd gone to a home, said it sadly, said he visited. Some of us believed him.\n\nWhat we know is that the brass plate is gone now. We asked once. Marcus said it was tarnishing. He said he'd have a new one made.\n\nOn Sundays a woman in a black dress sits close to the piano and mouths the words with her eyes closed. Recently widowed, we hear. Marcus brought her a drink she didn't order. We turned our faces politely toward the bar."
 },
 {
  "title": "Contact Sheet",
  "body": "The Sunset Dreams Lounge had a photographer on Fridays, an old-fashioned touch Hana liked. A young man named Pip with a film camera who took pictures of couples and sold the prints for ten dollars at the end of the night. Teddy, the owner, called it the house tradition. He said people had forgotten how to be looked at.\n\nHana had been divorced for five months. She had been looked at, lately, mostly by lawyers.\n\nPip took her picture the first Friday she came in alone. She laughed and covered her face and he took it anyway. At the end of the night Teddy brought her the print in a little paper sleeve. In the photo she was laughing into her hand and she looked about thirty-two. \"On the house,\" he said. \"Nobody should have to buy proof they're beautiful.\"\n\nShe put it on her refrigerator. She hadn't had anything on her refrigerator since Daniel took the magnets, which, he'd argued, were his mother's.\n\nShe came most Fridays after that. Pip photographed her at the bar, in the booth, dancing once with a man from out of town, and Teddy brought her the prints, always on the house, always with a sentence. \"Look at your shoulders here.\" \"That's a woman who's done apologizing.\" She started dressing for the camera. She did not quite admit it to herself.\n\nIn the spring Teddy asked if she'd like to see the archive. Upstairs, a narrow room with flat files and a light table. Forty years of Friday nights. He pulled out drawers and showed her strangers laughing in 1987, kissing in 1994. Then he opened a drawer with her name on the tab.\n\nThere were many more than she'd been given. Contact sheets. Frames she'd never seen: her checking her phone, her eyes swollen in the bathroom mirror through the half-open door, her asleep for a moment in the back booth, mouth open. Her talking to the man from out of town, closer than she remembered standing. Her in the parking lot, which she had not known was a place Pip went.\n\n\"I keep everything,\" Teddy said quietly. \"It's how I take care of people. Nobody ever loses a night.\"\n\nShe felt the room tilt. She said it was a lot. He agreed that it was. He said Daniel's lawyers would probably love an archive like this, wouldn't they, a woman alone in a bar every Friday, and then he laughed and closed the drawer and said, \"Not that anyone will ever see it. They're mine. They're safe with me.\"\n\nShe thanked him. She didn't know what else to say.\n\nShe still comes on Fridays. She sits where the light is good. Teddy brings her a print at the end of the night, on the house, always with a sentence, and she always says thank you, and she smiles at Pip when he raises the camera, because it would be strange not to, and because Teddy is watching to see if she does."
 },
 {
  "title": "In Reverse",
  "body": "Sunday. Joaquin stands behind the bar of the Sunset Dreams Lounge in a black vest that fits him perfectly, because Sal had it tailored, and he pours a bourbon for a man he used to cook for. The man does not recognize him. Sal, at the end of the bar, raises his glass an inch. Joaquin smiles back. He does not remember deciding to smile.\n\nTwo weeks before. The landlord's letter on the counter of the restaurant next door, which is no longer Joaquin's restaurant, which is no longer anyone's. Sal reads it over his shoulder and says, \"You don't owe me a thing. You know that.\" Joaquin knows that. Sal has told him a hundred times. Sal slides something across the bar. Not an apron. A vest.\n\nA month before. The health inspector, unannounced, on a Thursday. Somebody had called. Joaquin never found out who. Sal brought him a coffee afterward and sat with him on the curb and said, \"People are jealous of a man with a gift.\" He said it like he was angry on Joaquin's behalf. He paid the fine himself, wouldn't hear of anything else, and Joaquin cried on the curb, and Sal held the back of his neck.\n\nThree months before. The second loan, to replace the walk-in. Sal wrote the check at the bar with a fountain pen and wouldn't take a note. \"A handshake,\" he said. \"We're neighbors.\" He asked, while he was blotting the ink, whether Joaquin's sister had found work yet, and when Joaquin said no, Sal made a call, and she had a job by Monday at a hotel Sal knew. Joaquin's mother sent Sal a tin of polvorones.\n\nFive months before. Sal coming next door for the first time and ordering everything on the menu. Sending notes back to the kitchen on cocktail napkins, saying which dishes were genius and which were merely very good. He was always right. Joaquin kept the napkins in a drawer.\n\nSix months before. The first loan. Two thousand dollars, the week the produce supplier cut him off. \"Pay me back when it's easy,\" Sal said. \"And if it's never easy, it's never easy.\" Joaquin laughed. It was a relief to laugh.\n\nEight months before. Joaquin, closing late, smoking in the alley between the restaurant and the lounge. Sal came out the lounge's back door with two glasses and handed him one. \"I've been watching you,\" he said. \"The way you come out here after service like a man coming up for air.\" He said, \"You're too good for this block.\" Joaquin felt seen, which is a feeling like warmth, like being handed a drink in the cold.\n\nA year before. Sal at the front window of the lounge, watching a sign go up next door. A new restaurant. A young chef carrying crates in from a van, alone, sleeves rolled, whistling. Sal watched the whole afternoon. He sent the bartender over with a bottle of champagne and a card that said WELCOME, NEIGHBOR. He did not sign it. He wanted to see what the young man would do with something good he couldn't explain."
 },
 {
  "title": "Last Call",
  "body": "Every night at the Sunset Dreams Lounge ends the same way. At one-forty Conrad dims the house lights to a quarter. At one-forty-five he plays the same record, a Julie London side, and the regulars know it means finish up. At two he locks the front door and pours one drink, always for the same person, and the staff count out.\n\nWhen Noor started in August, the drink was for Bettina, who had waited tables there eleven years.\n\nNoor was saving for culinary school. She had a jar at home with a label that said PARIS, written in her own hand as a joke she half meant. She told Conrad on her first night, when he asked what she wanted out of life, and he didn't laugh. He asked which school. He asked what she'd cook first. He remembered the answer the next week. Tarte Tatin. Her grandmother's pan.\n\nHer schedule was good at first. Then it changed. Thursday doubles. Sunday closes. A shift on her day off, as a favor. Conrad apologized every time. He said she was the only one he trusted on a Saturday. He said Bettina was slowing down. He said it like a secret between them, and she kept it.\n\nIn October, at two in the morning, Conrad poured the drink for Noor.\n\nBettina was still counting her tips at the end of the bar. She did not look up. She folded her bills, stood, and put her coat on slowly, and on the way out she touched Noor's shoulder, very lightly, the way you touch someone at a funeral.\n\nNoor drank it. Rye, something sweet. Conrad watched her. He said she had good instincts. He said real chefs learn in rooms like this, not in Paris, not from people who'd never had to close. He said, \"You know what they'd teach you there? To be like everyone else.\"\n\nShe stayed later. The drink became two. He talked about the restaurant he'd open someday, upstairs, twelve seats, and he needed someone with instincts, and he looked at her when he said it.\n\nShe stopped putting money in the jar in November. There didn't seem to be a reason. She spent it on knives instead, good ones, a set he recommended, from a man he knew.\n\nBettina didn't come back after New Year's. Conrad said she'd moved to Phoenix, near her daughter. Noor texted her, and the message sat there, delivered, unanswered.\n\nIn February a new hire started. Lucía. She was studying for the nursing boards. She had a picture of her son taped inside her locker. Conrad asked her on her first night what she wanted out of life, and Noor, rolling silverware at the end of the bar, heard the softness come into his voice, the same exact softness, and felt something in her stomach that was not quite jealousy and not quite fear.\n\nAt one-forty the lights went down. At one-forty-five, Julie London. At two, the lock.\n\nConrad poured one drink. He held it a moment, just long enough for both of them to see him hesitate.\n\nThen he slid it to Noor. She took it. She was so grateful she could have wept."
 },
 {
  "title": "The Blackout",
  "body": "9:12 p.m. The grid goes down across the east side of the city, and the Sunset Dreams Lounge goes dark with it, mid-song. Someone screams, then laughs. Graham, who is in town for six weeks auditing a regional bank and has been drinking alone here for four of them, feels a hand on his forearm in the dark. \"Stay put,\" says Augustin. \"It's safer inside.\"\n\n9:30. Candles. Augustin has dozens, as if he's been waiting. He sets them on every table and the room turns gold and old-fashioned and everyone becomes better looking. Phones are at ten percent. Augustin says the bar is open, cash or tab, honor system, and people cheer.\n\n10:15. The street outside is black. A siren, far away. Augustin locks the front door and pockets the key. \"Looters,\" he says, apologetically. Nobody objects. There is a feeling in the room like a snow day.\n\n11:00. Augustin sits with Graham. He knows Graham's name; he has always known it. He asks about the bank. Graham shouldn't say anything and says a little. Augustin listens like it's the most interesting thing he's ever heard. He says, \"You're the only honest man in that building, aren't you.\" Graham hadn't thought of it that way.\n\n11:40. A couple asks to leave. Augustin unlocks the door for them, and the dark outside is so complete that they come back in after a minute, laughing nervously. Augustin locks it again. \"See,\" he says to Graham, as if it proves something.\n\n12:30 a.m. Graham has a dead phone and a warm drink. Augustin has told him about his late wife, about his bad heart, about how he has no one to leave the lounge to. He asks if Graham has ever thought about staying in a town like this. A smaller life. A man could buy into a place like this, he says. A man with a head for numbers.\n\n1:15. A woman at the bar, a regular, says something sharp to Augustin about the door. He smiles and refills her glass and says nothing, and ten minutes later she is crying in the restroom and nobody knows why. When she comes out she apologizes to him. He accepts.\n\n2:00. Augustin shows Graham the books. By candlelight, in the office, the ledger open on the desk. Something about it is wrong in a way Graham could find in an hour. Augustin says, \"I've never shown anyone this.\" He says, \"I trust you.\" He pours two more.\n\n3:30. Graham wakes on the leather couch in the office with his jacket over him like a blanket, the ledger closed, and Augustin sitting across from him, watching, perfectly composed. \"You talked a lot,\" Augustin says kindly. \"About the bank. About things you probably shouldn't have.\" He shrugs. \"Don't worry. I won't remember any of it if you don't.\"\n\n5:50. The power returns all at once, every light, the jukebox mid-song. Graham blinks at the brightness. Augustin unlocks the front door, holds it open for him, squeezes his shoulder, and says, \"Same time tomorrow?\"\n\nGraham says yes before he can think. The street is gray and ordinary. He has five weeks left in town."
 },
 {
  "title": "Forty",
  "body": "I knew she'd been stood up before she did. You learn. She came in at seven-thirty in a green dress that was too much for a Tuesday, told my bartender she was meeting someone, ordered two glasses of prosecco, and set the second one across from her like a placeholder. By eight-fifteen she'd turned her phone face down. By eight-forty she'd drunk the second glass.\n\nI sent over a slice of cake with a single candle. I didn't know it was her birthday. You'd be amazed how often it is.\n\nShe laughed and covered her mouth and looked around for who had done it, and I let her find me, and I raised my glass. Forty, she said when I came over. Forty and stood up at a bar called Sunset Dreams. You couldn't write it. I said I'd read worse. She laughed again. People like to laugh at the thing that's hurting them; you just have to give them permission.\n\nHer name was Celeste. She taught middle school orchestra. The man had been from an app. I told her I'd had my heart broken in this very room in 1991, which isn't true, but it was the right story, and she leaned in.\n\nI'm not a monster. I want to be clear about that. I make people feel good. That's my whole business. The only question is what it costs and who keeps the receipt.\n\nShe came back Thursday. I'd saved her table. I'd had my bartender learn her drink. When she sat down I said, \"Celeste,\" like it was a word I'd been practicing, and I watched her shoulders drop an inch. That inch is everything. I've built a life on that inch.\n\nI let her talk about the app men, the violin students, her sister who has three kids and a husband who brings her coffee in bed. I agreed her sister had it easy. I agreed the men were idiots. I agreed, gently, that most people didn't see her. Then I said the thing I always say, which is, \"I see you,\" and you'd think a grown woman would hear how cheap that is. They never do. Nobody's ever said it to them in a room this warm.\n\nBy spring she was here four nights a week. She'd stopped dating. Why bother, she said. She'd stopped calling her sister so much. Her sister was judgmental, I agreed. I gave her a key to the back door so she could come in early and play the piano when the room was empty. She wept when I handed it to her.\n\nI keep the key ring in my desk. There are eleven others on it.\n\nHer birthday's coming again. Forty-one. I'll have a cake. One candle. I'll tell her she doesn't look a day older and I'll mean it, because she doesn't; she looks exactly the way I want her to look, which is like someone who has nowhere else to go.\n\nYou're wondering why I'm telling you this. You came in alone, didn't you. You've been looking at your phone.\n\nLet me get you something. On the house."
 },
 {
  "title": "Top Shelf",
  "body": "Frank Delaney said he only came in for the bourbon, and for two years that was true. The Sunset Dreams Lounge kept a good shelf, and Rocco, who owned it, poured heavy and didn't talk, which was all Frank wanted from a bar on the way home from the plant.\n\nThe new bartender, Jules, talked.\n\nNot a lot. She asked how his day was and then she actually waited for an answer. That was new. Frank found himself telling her about his foreman. Then his daughter's tuition. Then, one night in March, after the third pour, about the woman from accounting he'd had lunch with twice, just lunch, nothing, but he'd liked it more than he should, and he hadn't mentioned it to Carol.\n\nJules nodded and wiped the bar. She didn't make a face. He went home lighter.\n\nThe next night Rocco poured him a bourbon he hadn't ordered, a better one, from the top shelf. He leaned on the bar for the first time in two years. \"Lunch,\" Rocco said. \"There's no crime in lunch.\" Then he winked and walked away.\n\nFrank sat very still.\n\nHe didn't come in for a week. Then he did, because Carol was angry about the tuition, because the plant was laying people off, because where else. Rocco greeted him by name. He poured the top shelf again. He asked about the foreman, by name. He asked about the daughter's school, by name. Every single thing Frank had told Jules, Rocco knew, and he said each one warmly, carefully, the way you'd hand back a man's wallet after he dropped it. Look what I'm holding for you.\n\nFrank stopped talking to Jules. Rocco talked to Frank instead. Rocco was good at it. He said, \"Your wife doesn't understand what that plant does to a man.\" He said, \"You deserve one place where nobody needs anything from you.\" Frank found he agreed. He began coming every night. He began calling Carol from the parking lot to say he was working late.\n\nIn June Rocco asked him for a favor. His nephew needed a job. Something at the plant. Frank was a supervisor now, after the layoffs; he'd survived them, and he'd half wondered why. Rocco had known a man at corporate. Rocco always knew a man.\n\nFrank hired the nephew. Then the nephew's friend. Then, in August, Rocco asked whether the loading dock was locked on Sundays, just curious, just a question, and Frank said he didn't know, and Rocco poured the top shelf and said, \"Find out for me.\"\n\nFrank looked at him for a long moment. Rocco looked back pleasantly.\n\n\"How's Carol?\" Rocco asked. \"Still doesn't know about lunch?\"\n\nThere had only been the two lunches. There had never been anything. But Frank could hear how it would sound in Rocco's voice, that warm, careful voice, and he understood suddenly that it would not matter what was true.\n\nHe found out about the dock. He came back the next night and told Rocco, quietly, and Rocco squeezed his hand on the bar and said, \"There he is.\" Jules was cutting limes. She did not look up."
 },
 {
  "title": "Now That Is a Mind",
  "body": "Thursday trivia at the Sunset Dreams Lounge had been running for twenty-two years, and Margaret Coyle's team, the Dewey Decimators, had won it nine times. Four retired teachers and a librarian. They sat at the round table under the mirror and brought their own pencils.\n\nDesmond took over the lounge in the spring, and the first thing he did was host trivia himself.\n\nHe was wonderful at it. Funny, quick, a little wicked. He wrote harder questions. He learned everyone's names in a week. He singled Margaret out the first night, after she got a question about Byzantine coinage nobody else in the room could touch. \"Now that,\" he said into the microphone, \"is a mind.\" The room applauded. She blushed like a girl.\n\nHe started asking her to check his questions in advance. Just her. He'd bring the cards to her table on Tuesdays and they'd go over them with coffee, and he deferred to her on everything, and she found herself looking forward to Tuesdays in a way she hadn't looked forward to anything since she retired.\n\nOne Tuesday he mentioned that Phyllis had been asking whether Margaret got the questions early. He said it with a little laugh. He said he'd told Phyllis of course not. \"But you know Phyllis,\" he said. Margaret did know Phyllis. Thirty years at the same school. She hadn't known Phyllis was the type.\n\nAt trivia that week Phyllis was cool to her. Margaret was cool back. The Decimators came in third.\n\nThe next Tuesday Desmond said Ruth had told him Margaret was getting a little forgetful. He said he'd defended her. He said, \"I told her, that woman knew Byzantine coinage cold.\" He said it to make her feel better and it did, and underneath it something else, a small cold stone, settled in her stomach and stayed.\n\nShe stopped meeting the Decimators for lunch. They stopped calling. By summer she was playing trivia at the bar, alone, on a team Desmond had named for her: Margaret Alone. He said it was a joke, affectionately. He announced her score first, before the teams. She won three weeks running. People clapped politely and didn't look at her.\n\nThe Decimators broke up in August. Phyllis's husband was sick; Ruth was moving. Margaret heard it from Desmond. \"They didn't tell you?\" he asked, very gently. \"I'm so sorry. I'd have thought.\"\n\nIn September he asked if she'd consider helping with the books. Just the bookkeeping. She'd been so good with the questions. A woman with her mind, wasted at home. She said yes before he finished.\n\nThursday nights she sits at the round table under the mirror by herself now. Desmond calls her the house champion. She has her own pencil. Sometimes, in the mirror, she sees a new face at another table answer something difficult, and she watches Desmond lean into the microphone and say, \"Now that is a mind,\" and she grips her pencil until her knuckles go white, and she doesn't know who she's angry at."
 },
 {
  "title": "The Members' Room",
  "body": "Councilwoman Teresa Vance came to the Sunset Dreams Lounge for the quiet. She told Gil that on her first night, and he nodded gravely and made sure no one ever approached her table. Not constituents, not reporters, not the occasional drunk who recognized her from the news. Gil handled it. A word, a hand at the elbow, a drink on the house, and they drifted away.\n\nShe had not had quiet in four years.\n\nIn the fall he showed her the room upstairs. A members' room, he called it, though she never saw a list of members. Low leather chairs, a fire, a window over the river. He gave her a brass key on a red tassel. \"For when the quiet downstairs isn't quiet enough,\" he said.\n\nShe went up alone at first. She read briefs by the fire. Gil would knock once, bring a pot of tea, and leave. Then he'd knock once and stay. He knew about zoning, it turned out. He knew about the riverfront development. He had opinions, careful ones, which he offered as questions.\n\nShe found herself saying things in that room she didn't say in chambers.\n\nOther people had keys. She learned that in December, when she came up and found a developer named Pruitt by the fire, a man who'd been in her office twice that month. Gil was pouring him a drink. Gil looked up and smiled with real warmth and said, \"Teresa, you know Pruitt.\" She said she did. She should have turned around. Gil had already poured her a glass.\n\nIt was a pleasant hour. Nothing was discussed. That was the strange part. Pruitt talked about his grandchildren, about sailing. Gil laughed at the right moments. When she stood to go, Gil helped her with her coat and said softly, \"See? People are only people up here.\"\n\nAfter that there were others. A union man. A judge. A woman from the state's attorney's office. Gil introduced her to each one like a gift. Never any business. Just the fire, the river, a drink, and the brass keys on their red tassels set on the side table like a little collection.\n\nIn March a reporter called her office. Was it true she'd met privately with Pruitt during the riverfront vote. She said no. The reporter said there was a photograph. She said that was impossible. The reporter said the photograph showed her by a fire, laughing, Pruitt's hand on her arm.\n\nShe went to the lounge that night and up the stairs, and Gil was waiting by the fire with tea already poured. He looked stricken. He said he'd find out who did it. He said he'd protect her. He said, \"You know I'd never let anything happen to you up here.\" He took her hand.\n\nShe believed him. That was what she could never explain to anyone afterward. She sat by the fire and let him hold her hand, and she believed him, and when he asked, very gently, how she planned to vote on the river parcel next month, she told him."
 },
 {
  "title": "Rides Home",
  "body": "October 3. Theo auditions for the house piano gig at the Sunset Dreams Lounge and plays too fast, the way he always does when he's nervous. Elias, the owner, listens from a barstool with his eyes closed. Afterward he says, \"You play like a man running from something.\" Then he says, \"Can I drive you home? It's raining.\" Theo's bike has a flat. Theo says yes.\n\nOctober 3, later. Elias's car is old and immaculate and smells of leather and cloves. He doesn't play the radio. He asks who taught Theo, and Theo tells him about Mrs. Lindqvist, about the conservatory he dropped out of, about his father, who called the piano a girl's thing until the day he died. Elias drives the long way. Theo notices and doesn't mind.\n\nOctober 10. Theo gets the gig. Elias drives him home again, though it isn't raining. \"Your bike's not safe on Ridge Avenue,\" he says. Theo doesn't argue.\n\nOctober 24. Elias stops outside Theo's building and doesn't unlock the doors right away. He says he's been thinking about Theo's father. He says some men don't know how to love what they don't understand. Theo sits in the dark car and something in his throat closes and opens. Elias unlocks the doors. He doesn't say good night. He says, \"Same time Friday.\"\n\nNovember 7. Theo mentions he's been asked to sub at a wedding, a good fee. Elias is quiet the whole drive. At the curb he says he's glad for him, really. He says the lounge can survive one Saturday without him. At the wedding Theo plays well and thinks about the silence in that car the entire time.\n\nNovember 8. Elias doesn't pick him up. Theo rides his bike on Ridge Avenue in the rain and gets clipped by a cab mirror, nothing serious, a bruise. When he gets to the lounge Elias sees the scrape on his arm and goes pale and takes him into the office and cleans it himself with a cloth and says, \"This is my fault. I should never have let you go.\" Theo apologizes. He isn't sure for what.\n\nNovember 21. Theo's bike is gone from the alley. Stolen, Elias says. Terrible neighborhood. He'll drive Theo every night now. It's settled.\n\nDecember 5. Theo asks to be dropped at a friend's place instead of home. Elias says sure. Elias waits outside the friend's building with the engine running for two hours. When Theo comes out, Elias has a coffee for him, still hot, and doesn't mention the time.\n\nDecember 19. Theo stops seeing the friend. It's easier.\n\nJanuary 9. Theo plays \"Für Elise\" between sets as a joke, the way Mrs. Lindqvist used to make him. Elias laughs harder than anyone. On the drive home he says, \"I'd never have called it a girl's thing.\" Theo feels warm for a mile.\n\nJanuary 9, later. Elias drives past Theo's building without slowing. Theo says, that's my stop. Elias says he knows. He says he just wants to drive a little longer. He says there's no one waiting up for either of them, is there, and Theo realizes it's true, and leans his head against the window, and lets the car carry him wherever it's going."
 },
 {
  "title": "Twenty-Seven",
  "body": "June and Walter Pike have come to the Sunset Dreams Lounge on every anniversary for twenty-six years. Same booth, same Manhattans, same song on the jukebox, which Walter plays with a quarter he keeps in his wallet all year for exactly that purpose.\n\nHarlan bought the lounge last spring. On their anniversary he sent over a bottle of champagne and a note in fountain pen: TO TWENTY-SIX MORE. June kept the note.\n\nThey started coming in more often. Harlan made it easy. He remembered them. He sat with them. He told stories about the old owner that made Walter laugh until he coughed. June hadn't heard Walter laugh like that since his retirement.\n\nThe first strange thing happened in September. Walter had three Manhattans instead of two and said something sharp about June's sister, and June said something sharp back, and they went home quiet. The next week Harlan sat down in their booth, and when Walter went to the restroom, Harlan put his hand over June's and said, \"Are you all right? After last week?\"\n\nJune said it was nothing. A spat. Harlan frowned in a kind way. \"He grabbed your wrist, June,\" he said softly. \"I saw it from the bar.\"\n\nShe was certain he hadn't. Almost certain. She looked at her wrist. Harlan squeezed her hand and let go before Walter came back.\n\nIn October Harlan stopped Walter at the coat check. June saw them talking, and on the drive home Walter said, out of nowhere, \"Harlan says you were flirting with the bartender.\" June laughed. Walter didn't. \"He says you do it every time I go to the restroom.\" June said that was insane. Walter said, \"Is it?\" and looked at the road.\n\nAfter that Harlan told each of them things. Quietly, separately, with great sorrow. What the other had said after a third drink. What the other had done when they thought nobody was looking. He was never cruel about it. He seemed to hurt for them. He always ended the same way: \"I just thought you should know. I care about you two.\"\n\nJune started writing things down in a little notebook in her purse, what she'd said, what she'd done, to have proof. She showed it to Harlan once, hoping he'd see. He read it carefully, then closed it and handed it back and said, \"June. Sweetheart. People who write things down are usually afraid of what they did.\"\n\nWalter moved into the guest room in December. Neither of them could have said exactly why.\n\nThey still come to the lounge. Separately, now. June on Tuesdays, Walter on Fridays. Harlan sits with each of them and listens and tells them they're not crazy, it's the other one, and they both feel understood for the first time in months.\n\nOn their twenty-seventh anniversary June came in alone and sat in the booth. Harlan sent over champagne. The note in fountain pen said TO YOU.\n\nSomebody had put a quarter in the jukebox. Their song was playing. Walter was at the end of the bar, not looking at her. Harlan stood between them, smiling, a hand on each of their shoulders, as if posing for a photograph."
 },
 {
  "title": "The Dome",
  "body": "Isaac wrote his poems at the end of the bar at the Sunset Dreams Lounge because it was the only place in the city he'd never been interrupted. Cyril, the owner, made sure of it. \"A poet needs a corner,\" Cyril said, and set a little brass lamp on the bar beside Isaac's notebook, and that was that.\n\nCyril read the poems sometimes. Only when invited. He was a good reader. He would sit with a page for a long time and then point at a single line and say, \"This. The rest is scaffolding.\" He was right more often than Isaac's workshop had been.\n\nIn the winter Cyril installed cameras. A string of black glass domes along the ceiling. A break-in down the block, he said. Everybody was doing it. Isaac noticed one directly over his corner, but it was just a dome, and he forgot about it.\n\nIn February Cyril showed him a clip on his phone. It was Isaac, in his corner, at one in the morning, face in his hands, shoulders shaking. Isaac didn't remember crying. He remembered a bad night, a rejection letter, the fourth bourbon.\n\n\"I didn't know whether to come over,\" Cyril said. \"I watched you for a while. Upstairs. You looked so alone.\" He put the phone away. \"I just want you to know someone sees you.\"\n\nIsaac was embarrassed, and touched, and something else, a small cold thing underneath that he decided not to look at.\n\nAfter that Cyril mentioned things. Little things, kindly. That Isaac chewed his pen when he was close to something good. That he mouthed his lines. That he'd looked at his ex-girlfriend's photos for twenty minutes on Tuesday. \"You should let her go,\" Cyril said. \"It's eating you.\" Isaac had not told anyone about the photos. He had not known the dome could see his screen.\n\nHe thought about writing somewhere else. He went to a coffee shop twice. It was bright and loud and nobody set a lamp out for him, and he wrote nothing. When he came back to the lounge Cyril didn't mention his absence. The lamp was lit. His drink was poured. He wrote three pages that night, good ones, and felt relief like a fever breaking.\n\nIn April Cyril asked to put a few of the poems on the wall. Framed. With Isaac's name. Isaac said yes, of course. It was the first time anything of his had hung anywhere.\n\nThey were the ones from the worst nights. The ones about his father, about the hospital. Cyril had chosen them himself. Isaac hadn't known Cyril had copies. He hadn't known what was in the notebook was something you could copy from a ceiling.\n\nPeople read them while they waited for drinks. Strangers. Sometimes they looked over at him in his corner, curious, sympathetic, the way you look at an exhibit.\n\nIsaac still writes there. He tilts the notebook away from the dome now. Cyril noticed. He brought over a fresh drink and said gently, \"You don't have to hide from me,\" and Isaac tilted it back."
 },
 {
  "title": "The Coat",
  "body": "The coat was the first thing Lorne gave Simone, three weeks after he hired her to tend bar at the Sunset Dreams Lounge. Charcoal wool, cut long, with a lining the color of a bruise. A customer had left it years ago, he said, and never come back for it. It fit her as if measured.\n\n\"You walk to the bus in that thin thing?\" he said. \"Not anymore.\"\n\nShe wore it home that night and felt like someone in a film.\n\nThe coat had deep pockets. In the first week she found a ticket stub for a concert in 1998 and a book of matches from a hotel in Montreal. She asked Lorne about the woman who'd left it. He said he couldn't remember. He said, \"She didn't need it anymore,\" and went to check on the ice.\n\nLorne was a fair boss. Better than fair. He paid in cash at the end of every night, generously, and when she was short on rent in her second month he covered it without a word, just tucked an envelope into the coat pocket while it hung on the hook. When she found it at the bus stop she cried a little.\n\nHe liked it when she wore the coat. He said so. On the nights she wore her old jacket he was quieter, a little sad, and the tips seemed thinner, and once he scheduled her for the dead Monday shift instead of Friday, and when she asked why he said he hadn't noticed. She started wearing the coat every day.\n\nIn winter he began walking her to the bus stop. Then driving her. He'd turn up the heat and say, \"You look like you belong in that coat.\" It sounded like a compliment. She was never quite sure.\n\nIn the third month a woman came into the lounge. Late forties, gray roots, a careful face. She ordered a club soda and stared at Simone the whole time she poured it. At the end she said, very quietly, \"Where did you get that?\"\n\nSimone looked down. She was wearing the coat. She always was now.\n\nLorne was beside her before she could answer. He was warm, delighted. He said, \"Mara. It's been a long time.\" He said the coat was a gift. He said, \"It looks better on her, doesn't it?\" He said it kindly. The woman's face did something Simone would spend months trying to describe. Then she put a five on the bar and left without her change.\n\n\"Who was that?\" Simone asked.\n\n\"Someone who used to work here,\" Lorne said. \"She had a hard time letting go of things.\"\n\nThat night, in the coat pocket, Simone found something new. A matchbook from the lounge, and inside the cover, in faded ink, in handwriting not her own: DON'T STAY PAST SPRING.\n\nShe held it a long time at the bus stop. Then Lorne's car pulled up, warm, the door already open, and she got in, and when he asked what she was holding she said nothing, nothing at all, and slid the matchbook back into the pocket where she'd found it, where it would keep."
 },
 {
  "title": "The List",
  "body": "I work the door at the Sunset Dreams Lounge on weekends. There's no cover and no line. Benedict keeps a list anyway, on a clipboard under the podium. Most nights it's just names. Some nights there are little marks beside them. A dot means let them in. A line means don't.\n\nPriya came in every Saturday with her friends. Four women from a law office, loud, happy, the kind of group that makes a room feel alive. Benedict liked them. He sent over flights of whatever they were drinking. He learned all four names in a night. He liked Priya best. Everyone could see it.\n\nIn April the marks started.\n\nFirst it was the friend named Dana. A line beside her name. I asked Benedict, and he said she'd been rude to a server. I hadn't seen it. I turned Dana away, politely, the next Saturday, and the group stood on the sidewalk confused, and then three of them came in without her, because it was raining, because what else were they going to do.\n\nBenedict sat with them that night. He apologized to Priya personally. He said he hated to do it. He said, \"Some people, you know, they bring a darkness into a room.\"\n\nTwo weeks later, a line by Kendra. Benedict said she'd been seen with a man who'd caused trouble. Then a line by the last one, Josie, no reason given. \"Just trust me,\" he said. I trusted him. That's the job.\n\nPriya came alone after that. She asked me about it once at the door, her voice tight, and I said it was above my pay grade, and she nodded like she already knew it would be.\n\nBenedict met her inside. He always met her inside now. He said her friends had been jealous of her. He said it was obvious, the way they talked over her. He said she'd outgrown them, and she looked at him like he'd lifted something heavy off her back.\n\nShe stopped answering their texts. I know because one of them, Dana, came by the door on a Tuesday, sober, and asked me to give Priya a note. I said I would. I gave it to Benedict. I don't know what he did with it.\n\nIn August Priya's name had a little star next to it. I'd never seen a star before. I asked Benedict what it meant.\n\n\"It means she's family,\" he said.\n\nI let her in every night after that. She doesn't stay at the bar anymore. She goes straight back to the office with him, and the door closes. Sometimes, near close, she comes out and stands with me at the podium and looks at the street, at the people walking by in groups, laughing. She never says anything. She just watches them until they're gone.\n\nLast Saturday four young women came up the sidewalk. Nurses, by the look of them. Loud, happy. The one in the middle laughed at something, and the others leaned in toward her.\n\nBenedict came out and stood beside me and watched them for a long moment.\n\nThen he took the clipboard, wrote all four names down carefully, and handed it back to me without a single mark.\n\nNot yet."
 },
 {
  "title": "The Glass",
  "body": "It's been nine years since I last walked into the Sunset Dreams Lounge, and I still can't drink out of a coupe glass. My husband thinks it's a quirk. I let him.\n\nMaurice had one waiting for me. That's how it began. My third visit, a winter Friday. I was thirty-four and newly transferred to the city and didn't know a soul, and when I sat down at the bar there was a coupe already set out on a little linen square, etched with a crescent moon. \"Yours,\" Maurice said. \"If you want it.\"\n\nI asked why me. He said I had the face of someone who'd been the new girl a lot. I had. Army family. Eleven schools. I'd never told him that.\n\nThe glass lived on a shelf behind the bar, apart from the others. I'd watch the bartender reach for it when I came in, before I'd even sat down, and something in me that had been clenched since grade school loosened a little. A place where my glass was waiting.\n\nMaurice filled it himself on the nights he was in. He'd ask about work, my boss, the apartment with the radiator that knocked. He always remembered. He fixed the radiator, actually, sent a man over, wouldn't hear of payment. He said the city could be cold to people who didn't have anyone.\n\nI had people, at first. A woman from work. A man I'd met at a gallery. Maurice was lovely to them when I brought them in. Afterward he'd say small things. That the woman from work seemed competitive with me. That the man from the gallery had looked at the bartender a lot. He'd say it and then wave it off: \"I'm overprotective. Ignore me.\" I couldn't ignore him. That was the whole design.\n\nBy the second winter it was mostly just Maurice and me and the glass.\n\nThen one night I came in and the glass wasn't on the shelf. The bartender gave me an ordinary coupe. I looked down the bar and saw mine, the crescent moon catching the light, in front of a young woman I'd never seen, new in town by the look of her, holding it with both hands like something precious.\n\nMaurice found me later. He was sorry. He'd had to lend it out, just for the night. He said, \"You understand. You of all people.\" He said, \"Don't make this something it's not.\"\n\nI stood in that room and felt eleven schools' worth of lunchrooms go quiet around me. I would have done anything to get that glass back. I knew that, standing there. I could feel exactly how much.\n\nThat was the night I left, and I didn't go back, and it took me two years to stop driving past.\n\nI'm telling you because last week my niece moved to the city. Thirty-one, knows no one. She texted me a picture from a lounge downtown, amber light, a neon sign. A coupe glass on a linen square, etched with a moon.\n\n\"Look,\" she wrote. \"They gave me my own glass.\""
 },
 {
  "title": "Index Cards",
  "body": "Leon keeps a box of index cards in the office of the Sunset Dreams Lounge. One card per regular. He writes in pencil so things can change.\n\nFIRST VISIT. Came in alone, 9:40. Sat at the bar but angled toward the door. Ordered something they didn't really want so they'd have something to hold. Checked phone eleven times. No one wrote back. Note: likes to be asked a question and then left alone to answer it.\n\nSECOND VISIT. Same stool. Had the bartender make the same drink without asking. Watched the face. A little flush, pleased, trying not to be. Note: wants to be known but is embarrassed by wanting it. Good.\n\nTHIRD VISIT. Sat with them six minutes. Asked about work. They said it was fine. Waited. They said it wasn't fine, actually. Waited again. They told me about the manager, the one who takes credit. Said: \"People like that can smell talent. It scares them.\" Note: they repeated it back to me later, in their own words, as if it were their idea.\n\nFIFTH VISIT. Brought a friend. Friend talked too much, asked where I was from, didn't like the drinks. Comped the table anyway. When the friend went to the restroom, said quietly: \"You seem different when they're here.\" Note: friend has not been back. Subject has not mentioned them.\n\nEIGHTH VISIT. Told me about their mother. The phone calls. The way she says \"I'm just worried about you.\" Said: \"Some people love you in a way that keeps you small.\" Watched them nearly cry. Didn't hand them a napkin. Note: they reached for it themselves and looked at me after, to see whether I'd noticed they were strong. I had. I told them so.\n\nTENTH VISIT. Gave them a reserved card for their stool. Gold script. Watched them touch it.\n\nTWELFTH VISIT. Late. Upset about something at home. Didn't ask what. Said: \"You don't have to explain anything here.\" Locked the door after last call and let them stay until three. Talked about nothing. Note: they called it the best night they'd had in a year.\n\nFOURTEENTH VISIT. Asked them for a small favor. They said yes before I finished. Note: next time ask for something larger, and look embarrassed to be asking.\n\nFIFTEENTH VISIT. Missed a Friday. Sent nothing. When they came in Saturday, stayed at the other end of the bar for an hour. Let them watch me laugh with someone new. When I finally came over, they apologized. For what, they couldn't say. Note: the apology is the important part.\n\nThere's one more card in the box, behind all the others. It's blank except for the date.\n\nYou found this place on your own. That's what you'll tell yourself later. Nobody led you here. You pressed a button because you were curious, and when it asked whether you were sure, you said yes. Twice. More than twice, maybe. You keep coming back.\n\nLeon has started a card for you.\n\nHe hasn't decided yet what you like to drink. But he's noticed that you came in alone, that you've stayed, and that you haven't looked away once, and he's writing that down in pencil, carefully, so it can change."
 }
];
